{"candidates":[{"content":"Replace the per-node greedy DefenseWitness materialization with a canonical witness that stores, for each accepted node x, the complete set of accepted defenders that defeat each attacker of x, encoded as a bitset over the finite argument set. This set is uniquely determined by the grounded fixpoint and does not depend on enumeration order, so it avoids SRC_008's refutation. It is still a valid defense because every attacker is defeated by at least one member of the set; no minimality is claimed. Polynomial cost holds because each node's attacker-defender relation is already computed during fixpoint and bitset operations are linear in graph size. Criticism: full sets can be large, and the witness may include redundant defenders, but this is acceptable if the witness is not required to be minimal.","typicality":0.65,"neighbours":["SRC_003","SRC_008"]},{"content":"Make attack-defeater warrants themselves subject to the grounded fixpoint before they are allowed to defeat an attack. A registered warrant w that targets an attack edge e becomes effective only if w is accepted in the grounded extension of the framework that contains all warrants but not their defeats; then compute a second pass where those accepted warrants' defeats are applied. This avoids self-defeating warrants and keeps determinism because the two passes are each deterministic fixpoints. Criticism: circular dependencies between warrant acceptance and defeat application may require a careful stratification; a warrant that is only accepted because of its own defeat is rejected.","typicality":0.55,"neighbours":["SRC_002","SRC_006"]},{"content":"Extend the three-valued grounded labels to four values by adding a bilattice component that distinguishes 'undecided by lack of evidence' from 'undecided by conflict', while keeping the accepted/refuted projections equal to the original extension. The fourth label is computed by a monotone operator on the bilattice, so the overall fixed point remains deterministic and polynomial. This gives richer diagnostics without changing committed roots. Criticism: the new truth value may not correspond to any intended argumentation semantics, and proving the operator is monotone in both lattice orders is nontrivial.","typicality":0.4,"neighbours":["SRC_004","SRC_007"]},{"content":"Instead of a sequential witness scan, produce a root-ledger using reduced ordered binary decision diagrams (BDDs) where each committed root's full defense condition is represented as a canonical BDD. Because BDD reduction is canonical, the resulting witness is independent of enumeration order, and for the class of defense conditions expressible as read-once formulas the BDD size is polynomial. The ledger records only the BDD for each root, and verification is by checking that the BDD is non-empty under the grounded labels. Criticism: BDD blow-up is possible outside the read-once fragment, and importing BDD machinery may violate the spirit of the original lightweight spec.","typicality":0.2,"neighbours":["SRC_003","SRC_005","SRC_008"]},{"content":"Reframe grounded semantics as the least model of a Horn program whose predicates are `accepted/1`, `defeated/1`, and `attacks/2`, with rules such as `defeated(A) :- accepted(D), attacks(D,A)` and `accepted(X) :- not attacked_by_undefeated(X)` encoded via auxiliary predicates. Then compute the least model using unit propagation in polynomial time. Strengthen the extension by adding integrity constraints that reject any model where a committed root lacks a derivation; these constraints are checked during propagation, not after, so no invalid root is ever committed. Criticism: Horn encodings of non-monotonic defeat require extra negated predicates and may not preserve the exact grounded semantics unless carefully stratified.","typicality":0.45,"neighbours":["SRC_005","SRC_006"]},{"content":"Allow a user-supplied total preorder on attack edges, and define a strengthened grounded extension by deleting an attack edge if its target has an accepted defender with a higher-priority attack on the attacker. If the preorder is acyclic (e.g., derived from a deterministic timestamp), the deletion process is confluent and polynomial, and the resulting extension contains the original grounded extension. Reinstatement is then a derived property of the priority-deleted subgraph. Criticism: preferences are not part of the original specification and may make the semantics non-argumentative; also need to show that the original grounded extension is preserved in all cases.","typicality":0.3,"neighbours":["SRC_001","SRC_007"]}]}